        "Ruth Okafor had never thought of herself as a scientist. For twenty years she had worked as an accountant in a busy office, and numbers to her meant invoices and tax forms. But when an injury kept her at home for several months, her daughter, a university student, showed her a website that was asking volunteers for help. On the screen was a photograph of a page from a ship’s logbook written in 1871: neat columns of handwriting recording the date, the ship’s position, the air pressure, the temperature and the direction of the wind.",
